    <h2>The Science of Aggregate Bulk Density</h2>
    <p>
      In the aggregate supply industry, <strong>bulk density</strong> (historically termed "unit weight") defines the weight of aggregate particles per unit of volume, including the open air spaces (voids) between individual stones. Bulk density is universally determined in North America per <strong>ASTM C29 / C29M</strong> (<em>Standard Test Method for Bulk Density and Voids in Aggregate</em>) and <strong>AASHTO T 19</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Understanding bulk density is vital because materials are bought and sold across two different physical metrics: landscape drawings specify <strong>volume (cubic yards)</strong>, whereas quarries bill clients by <strong>weight (short tons of 2,000 pounds)</strong> across certified platform scales.
    </p>

